{
  "produced_by": "voice_agent",
  "source_narration_script": "You had one big project today. Instead you answered twelve emails, booked a dentist appointment, cleared three errands. By six you feel like you worked hard. The thing that mattered? Untouched. That wasn't laziness. Your list did get shorter. But a short list and a finished project aren't the same thing. Researchers once asked people to carry one of two heavy buckets down a hallway. One sat beside them. The other waited near the finish. The far bucket was the easier carry. Almost everyone grabbed the near one and lugged it the whole way. Extra effort, just to start doing something a few seconds sooner. There's a name for it: pre-crastination. Your busy day ran on the same instinct. You were counting tasks, not weighing them. Decide what you're trying to win. Or the easy wins eat your day.",
